 "notes": [
  {
   "id": "np2-01",
   "cat": "grammar",
   "label": "Conditional",
   "quote": "si … sederet … si non pertaesum … fuisset … potui",
   "trans": "if it were not … if I had not … I could have",
   "line": "15–19",
   "body": "Two \"if not\" clauses hold back the main verb. Dido builds her defences first, then admits in a single word, potui, that she could have given in.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np2-02",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Pairing",
   "quote": "fixum immotumque",
   "trans": "fixed and unmoved",
   "line": "15",
   "body": "Two near-synonyms stress how firm her resolve is, or how firm she wants it to sound. The reader soon sees it shaken.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np2-03",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Word choice",
   "quote": "vinclo … iugali",
   "trans": "the bond of marriage",
   "line": "16",
   "body": "Marriage is described as a chain or yoke. Dido presents it as something binding, which is what she fears and desires at once.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np2-04",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Personification",
   "quote": "primus amor deceptam morte fefellit",
   "trans": "my first love cheated and deceived me by dying",
   "line": "17",
   "body": "Her first love is spoken of as a person who tricked her. deceptam … fefellit doubles the idea of betrayal, a theme that will return with Aeneas.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np2-05",
   "cat": "sound",
   "label": "Alliteration",
   "quote": "thalami taedaeque",
   "trans": "the marriage chamber and the torch",
   "line": "18",
   "body": "The repeated t sounds stress her weariness of everything to do with marriage; the torch is the wedding torch that led the bride to her new home.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np2-06",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Euphemism",
   "quote": "huic uni … culpae",
   "trans": "to this one fault",
   "line": "19",
   "body": "Dido calls her love a culpa, a fault or sin, before anything has happened. huic uni tries to make it small: just this one failing.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np2-07",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Emphasis",
   "quote": "succumbere",
   "trans": "to give way",
   "line": "19",
   "body": "A verb of physical collapse, \"to sink beneath\". Love is pictured as a force that overpowers her.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np2-08",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Pathos",
   "quote": "miseri … Sychaei coniugis",
   "trans": "of my poor husband Sychaeus",
   "line": "20–21",
   "body": "miseri shows her continuing pity and love for Sychaeus, which makes her new feelings all the more painful to admit.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np2-09",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Vivid detail",
   "quote": "sparsos fraterna caede penates",
   "trans": "the household gods spattered with a brother's bloodshed",
   "line": "21",
   "body": "The murder is pictured through the blood on the family gods: a crime against the home itself, committed by her own brother.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np2-10",
   "cat": "diction",
   "label": "Emphasis",
   "quote": "solus hic",
   "trans": "this man alone",
   "line": "22",
   "body": "solus stresses that only Aeneas has moved her, which makes her confession more complete and more dangerous.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np2-11",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Metaphor",
   "quote": "animumque labantem impulit",
   "trans": "he has pushed my tottering heart",
   "line": "22–23",
   "body": "Her heart is pictured as a wall already slipping, and Aeneas has given it the push. The run-on to impulit enacts the push.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np2-12",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Fire imagery",
   "quote": "agnosco veteris vestigia flammae",
   "trans": "I recognise the traces of the old flame",
   "line": "23",
   "body": "Her love for Sychaeus was a flame; now she feels its marks again. The famous line is a moment of painful self-knowledge.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np2-14",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Pairing",
   "quote": "vel tellus … dehiscat vel pater omnipotens adigat",
   "trans": "either may the earth gape … or the almighty father drive",
   "line": "24–25",
   "body": "Two terrible alternatives, one from below and one from above, show how strongly she means her oath.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np2-15",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Repetition",
   "quote": "ad umbras, pallentes umbras",
   "trans": "to the shades, the pale shades",
   "line": "25–26",
   "body": "umbras is picked up and expanded at once, so the reader lingers in the darkness of the underworld, where Dido will end the book.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np2-16",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Apostrophe",
   "quote": "pudor",
   "trans": "Honour",
   "line": "27",
   "body": "Dido turns and speaks directly to her own sense of honour as if it were a goddess. Breaking faith with it becomes a kind of sacrilege.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np2-17",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Tragic irony",
   "quote": "ante, pudor, quam te violo",
   "trans": "before I violate you, Honour",
   "line": "27",
   "body": "Dido swears she would rather die than break her vow. She will break it, and she will die: the oath is a curse on herself.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np2-18",
   "cat": "structure",
   "label": "Repetition",
   "quote": "ille … ille",
   "trans": "that man … that man",
   "line": "28–29",
   "body": "ille at the start of both clauses points emphatically to Sychaeus. She tries to hand her love back to him in his grave.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np2-19",
   "cat": "imagery",
   "label": "Pathos",
   "quote": "sinum lacrimis implevit obortis",
   "trans": "she filled her lap with welling tears",
   "line": "30",
   "body": "Her speech ends not in resolve but in tears, which shows how weak her defences really are.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np2-20",
   "cat": "note",
   "label": "Sychaeus",
   "quote": "Sychaei",
   "trans": "of Sychaeus",
   "line": "20",
   "body": "Dido's first husband, a wealthy priest of Tyre, murdered for his money by her brother Pygmalion.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np2-21",
   "cat": "note",
   "label": "Erebus",
   "quote": "Erebi",
   "trans": "of Erebus",
   "line": "26",
   "body": "The darkness of the underworld, here a name for the land of the dead itself.",
   "exam": ""
  },
  {
   "id": "np2-22",
   "cat": "note",
   "label": "The almighty father",
   "quote": "pater omnipotens",
   "trans": "the almighty father",
   "line": "25",
   "body": "Jupiter, whose thunderbolt (fulmine) was the classic punishment for those who broke oaths.",
   "exam": ""
  }
 ],
 "section": 2,
 "questions": [
  "Dido gives two reasons for resisting her feelings: her vow and her pudor. How convincing does she find them herself?",
  "Compare the oath in lines 24–27 with what happens at the end of Book 4. What does Virgil gain by having Dido call down her own destruction here?",
  "\"agnosco veteris vestigia flammae\" (line 23). Why is this line so powerful, and what does it reveal about Dido?"
 ],
 "context": [
  {
   "h": "The univira",
   "body": "<p>Romans especially admired the <em>univira</em>, a woman who had only ever had one husband and did not remarry after his death. It was an ideal more than a rule, and remarriage was common in practice, but loyalty to a dead husband was praised on tombstones and in literature.</p><p>Dido has vowed to stay faithful to Sychaeus. Her <em>pudor</em> is more than modesty: it is her sense of honour and of what she owes to him and to her own good name. Breaking that vow is the step that the whole tragedy turns on, and she knows it.</p>"
  }
 ]
}